          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em Grothendieck topology
            on a category is a choice of morphisms in that category which are regarded as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} covers
          %p
            A category equipped with a Grothendieck topology is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
          %p
            Probably the main point of having a site is so that one can define
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaves
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stacks
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
          %h2#original_definition Original definition
          %p
            In the original definition (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Artin"}> Michael Artin
            ‘s seminar notes “Grothendieck topologies”), a Grothendieck topology on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is defined as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverings"} coverings
            satisfying certain closure properties.
          %p More precisely (to cite from Artin’s notes):
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong Grothendieck topology
              on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              of families of maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\phi_i\colon U_i\to U\}_{i\in I}
              (known as
              = succeed ")" do
                %strong coverings
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  for any
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                  we have
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi T
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\phi\}\in T
              %li
                %p
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi i
                        %mo →
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i\to U\}\in T
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mrow
                            %mi i
                            %mo ,
                            %mi j
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{V_{i,j}\to U_i\}\in T
                  for each
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %mo ,
                              %mi j
                          %mo →
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi T
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{V_{i,j}\to U\}\in T
              %li
                %p
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi i
                        %mo →
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i\to U\}\in T
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                        %mo →
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\to U
                  is a morphism, then
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi i
                        %msub
                          %mo ×
                          %mi U
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i\times_U V
                  exist and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mo ×
                            %mi U
                          %mi V
                          %mo →
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi T
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i\times_U V\to V\}\in T
            %p
          %p
            This is almost identical to the current definition of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+pretopology"} Grothendieck pretopology
          %h2#definition_using_sieves Definition using sieves
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi d
                  %mo →
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:d\to c
            is a morphism in a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\subset C(-,c)
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sieve"} sieve
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            then
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %mi dom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  g^* F = \{ h: dom(h)\to d | g\circ h \in F\}\subset C(-,d)
          %p
            is a sieve on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
          %p
            The following definition can be found in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SGA"} SGA
            4, Exposé II.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong Grothendieck topology
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
              on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is an assignment to each object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C
              of a collection of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sieves"} sieves
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              which are called
              = succeed "," do
                %em covering sieves
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  (Stability under
                  = succeed ".)" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sieve"} sieve
                  that covers
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo :
                        %mi d
                        %mo →
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: d \to c
                  is any morphism, then the pullback sieve
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi g
                          %mo *
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^* F
                  covers
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
              %li
                %p
                  (Local character condition.) If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  is a sieve on
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                  such that the sieve
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                          %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi g
                        %mo :
                        %mi d
                        %mo →
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %msup
                          %mi g
                          %mo *
                        %mi F
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi covers
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup_d \{g: d \to c| g^* F \; covers \; d\}
                  contains a covering sieve of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi c
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  itself covers
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi c
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+sieve"} maximal sieve
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi id
                        %mo :
                        %mi hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id: \hom(-, c) \hookrightarrow \hom(-, c)
                  is always a covering sieve;
          %p
            The set of covering sieves of an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            is denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J(c)
          %p
            A category equipped with a Grothendieck topology is called a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            \.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The axiom of stability under
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
              guarantees that we have a functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J: C^{op} \to Set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
              itself can be regarded as an object of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+topos"} presheaf topos
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C^{op},Set]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              are identified with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere-Tierney+topologies"} Lawvere-Tierney topologies
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C^{op},Set]
          %p
            Given a Grothendieck topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(C,J)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            relative to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
            of the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C^{op},Set]
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to Sh(C,J)
            given by the composite of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            with the reflection (or “sheafification”). This composite functor is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} fully faithful
            if and only if all representable presheaves are sheaves for
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcanonical+site"} subcanonical
          %h2#Saturation Saturation
          %p Grothendieck topologies may be and in practice quite often are obtained as closures of collections of morphisms that are not yet closed under the operations above (that are not yet sieves, not yet pullback stable, etc.).
          %p Two notions of such unsaturated collections of morphisms inducing Grothendieck topologies are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+pretopology"}> Grothendieck pretopology
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"}> coverage
                \.
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %h3#topology_on_open_subsets_of_a_topological_space Topology on open subsets of a topological space
          %p
            The archetypical example of a Grothendieck topology is that on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+open+subsets"} category of open subsets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset X
            is a collection of open subsets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi i
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_i \subset U
            that cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            in the ordinary sense of the word, i.e. which are such that every point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in U
            is in at least one of the
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_i
          %h3#regular_topology Regular topology
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            admits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcanonical+site"} subcanonical
            Grothendieck topology whose covering families are generated by single
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+epimorphisms"} regular epimorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exact
            or has pullback-stable
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+coequalizer"} reflexive coequalizer
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain+fibration"} codomain fibration
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            for this topology (the necessary and sufficient condition is that any pullback of a kernel pair is again a kernel pair).
          %h3#extensive_topology Extensive topology
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+category"} extensive category
            admits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} covering families
            are (generated by) the families of inclusions into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            (finite or small, as appropriate). We call this the
            %strong
              extensive
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %strong extensive topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain+fibration"} codomain fibration
            of any extensive category is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            for its extensive topology.
          %h3#coherent_topology Coherent topology
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+category"} coherent category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            admits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcanonical+site"} subcanonical
            Grothendieck topology in which the covering families are generated by finite, jointly
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+epimorphism"} regular-epimorphic
            families. Equivalently, they are generated by single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+epimorphisms"} regular epimorphisms
            and by finite unions of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+category"} extensive
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superextensive+site"} superextensive
          %h3#canonical_topology Canonical topology
          %p
            On any category there is a largest subcanonical topology. This is called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+topology"} canonical topology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
          %h2#historical_note_on_terminology Historical note on terminology
          %p
            The term “topology” for this concept is hallowed by tradition and the name of Grothendieck, but some people consider it to be somewhat unfortunate. While “a category equipped with a (Grothendieck) topology” is, in fact, sort of a generalization of “a set equipped with an (ordinary) topology,” the relationship between a category and its topology is quite different from the relationship between a set and its topology. In particular, when we construct a site from a topological space, the objects of the category are the
            = succeed "," do
              %em open sets
          %p
            This use of “topology” can also lead to confusion since for a topologist, there is a completely different and very natural meaning of “topology on a category:” namely, topologies on its sets of objects and morphisms making it into an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal category
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
          %p
            Furthermore, the use of “topology” for a category with a system of covers also leads to the use of “continuous” for a functor which preserves covers. This is (in some people’s opinion) doubly unfortunate, since “continuous functor” is used not only by topologists to mean an internal or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            functor over Top, but also by many category-theorists to mean a functor that preserves all small
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limits
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
            %strong coverage
            used above for a system of covers on a category, with “Grothendieck coverage” as a proposed replacement for “Grothendieck topology.” See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            for his definitions. Proposed replacements for “Lawvere-Tierney topology” include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em Local operator
                (used by Johnstone).
            %li
              %p
                %em Local modality
                or
                = succeed "," do
                  %em geometric modality
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
                of the topos, it represents a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operator"} modal operator
                with the intutive meaning of “it is locally the case that…”.
            %li
              %p
                %em Lawvere-Tierney operator
                or
                %em Lawvere-Tierney modality
                to avoid possible conflict with other uses of “local” or “geometric” in modal logic.
          %h2#related_notions Related notions
          %p
            A more general notion is simply a collection of “covering families,” not necessarily sieves, satisfying only pullback-stability; this suffices to define an equivalent notion of sheaf. Following the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"} Elephant
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            for details.
          %p
            An intermediate notion is that of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+pretopology"} Grothendieck pretopology
          %p
            As remarked above, Grothendieck topologies on a small category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            are also in bijective correspondence with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere-Tierney+topology"} Lawvere-Tierney topologies
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C^{op},Set]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere-Tierney+topology"} Lawvere-Tierney topology
            for a description of the correspondence.
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"}> coverage
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere-Tierney+topology"} Lawvere-Tierney topology
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+pretopology"} Grothendieck pretopology
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Q-category"} Q-category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cd-structure"} cd-structure
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weiss+topology"} Weiss topology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Grothendieck+topology"} (∞,1)-Grothendieck topology
          %h2#references References
          %p The original definition is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Artin"}> Michael Artin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Grothendieck Topologies. Notes on a Seminar by M. Artin. Spring, 1962
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.nagoya-u.ac.jp/~larsh/teaching/S2013_AG/grothendiecktopologies.pdf"} PDF
          %p
            The definition in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sieves"} sieves
            is found in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SGA"} SGA
              4: Théorie des topos et cohomologie étale des schémas.
          %p Standard books include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sheaves+in+Geometry+and+Logic"} Sheaves in Geometry and Logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Categorical+Algebra"} Handbook of Categorical Algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Ana Luiza Tenório, Hugo Luiz Mariano.
              %em Grothendieck prelopologies: towards a closed monoidal sheaf category
              (2024). (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.12313"}> arXiv:2404.12313
              ).
